From: virtio-gpu-desktop <setup@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] EXP input: pointer constraints on the subsurface under the pointer

A client may ask for a pointer lock or confinement on any of its surfaces (pointer-constraints-
unstable-v1). KWin activates a constraint only on the focused window's main surface, so a client
whose input surface is a subsurface - an app that embeds another client's view as a subsurface of
its window, e.g. vmview showing a VM's desktop inside a Qt window (route B, 2026-10-13) - can never
lock the pointer (the VM's Ctrl+Alt+G grab).

The constrained surface is now the input surface under the pointer (the main surface's
mapToInputSurface(), as the seat finds it for events) or the nearest of its parents with a
constraint of its own (an embedded view's surface may have subsurfaces of its own: the nested
desktop's window renders into a subsurface of its toplevel); without one it stays the main surface,
so a confinement of the main surface stays while the pointer crosses its subsurfaces. The
constrained surface's local position is used for the region tests, and where it lies in the main
surface now (mapToChild, live: its parents' commits move it) maps back for the lock hint and in
applyPointerConfinement(). The commits of the subsurface under the pointer and of its parents are
watched, so a constraint one of them commits takes effect at once; constraints are re-evaluated when
the pointer moves onto another surface of the active window, and a subsurface's constraint is broken
with the window's when the pointer leaves the window. Without subsurface constraints nothing changes.

Against KWin 6.7.91 (src/pointer_input.cpp, pointer_input.h); reviewed by the desktop session
(2026-10-13). Builds with sets/hl (bench/ab/kwin_hlg_build_1013.sh). Installed on the desktop host
(set hl39) 2026-10-14 02:19: vmview's grab (Ctrl+Alt+G in the guest) locks at once and holds, releases at
once (bench/ab/vmview_grab_test_1014.sh grv1); the nested desktop's own window unchanged (grv0).
